<<<
e.g. counsel, deer, grouse, reindeer, salmon, sheep, trout, (Many counsel were briefed; The grouse were shy; We saw no deer).
===
e.g. *counsel, deer, grouse, reindeer, salmon, sheep, trout, (Many counsel were briefed; The grouse were shy; We saw no deer).*
>>>

<<<
2. Words having a plural, but
===
**2.** Words having a plural, but
>>>

<<<
e.g. shot, cannon, (The shot scatters too much; Three shot were extracted from his head).
===
e.g. *shot, cannon, (The shot scatters too much; Three shot were extracted from his head).*
>>>

<<<
3. Words of number or amount
===
**3.** Words of number or amount
>>>

<<<
e.g. brace, dozen, hundred, fathom, pound, hundredweight, (We shot 20 brace; Six fathom deep; A few hundredweight of coal; Six pound of lard).
===
e.g. *brace, dozen, hundred, fathom, pound, hundredweight, (We shot 20 brace; Six fathom deep; A few hundredweight of coal; Six pound of lard).*
>>>

<<<
4. Names of an animal or vegetable that can have a & mean an individual, or be used in the singular without a & mean the things as food
===
**4.** Names of an animal or vegetable that can have *a* & mean an individual, or be used in the singular without *a* & mean the things as food
>>>

<<<
e.g. salmon, lamb, pig, grouse, potato, cabbage, (Went out sticking pig; Have some potato; Cabbage is a blood-purifier).
===
e.g. *salmon, lamb, pig, grouse, potato, cabbage, (Went out sticking pig; Have some potato; Cabbage is a blood-purifier).*
>>>

<<<
5. Words having no plural, but
===
**5.** Words having no plural, but
>>>

<<<
e.g. cattle (The c. is sold; The c. are in the hay).
===
e.g. *cattle (The c. is sold; The c. are in the hay).*
>>>

<<<
For the many blunders occasioned by these words (The German Government acknowledge him as its official courier) see PERSONIFICATION.
===
For the many blunders occasioned by these words *(The German Government* acknowledge *him* as its *official courier)* see ^^PERSONIFICATION^^.
>>>

<<<
to use c. in the same way as our
===
to use *c.* in the same way as our
>>>

<<<
grandfathers are laughed at for using academy. See WORKING & STYLISH WORDS.
===
grandfathers are laughed at for using *academy.* See ^^WORKING & STYLISH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
+ collie, colly. See -EY, -IE, -y.
===
**collie, colly.** See ^^-EY^^, ^^-IE^^, ^^-Y^^.
>>>

<<<
collocutor, colloquist, interlocutor, are rival candidates
===
**collocutor, colloquist, interlocutor,** are rival candidates
>>>

<<<
to stand for the other speaker, the person who was talking to & being talked to by me, you, him, or her.
===
to stand for *the other speaker, the person who was talking to & being talked to by* me, you, him, or her.
>>>

<<<
the third would have a_ better chance, & collocutor (k6'lokiiter) seems the best.
===
the third would have a better chance, & *collocutor* (kŏ'lokūter) seems the best.
>>>

<<<
collusion &c. 1. Pronounce -166- ; see LU. 2. The notion of fraud or underhandedness is essential to
===
**collusion &c.** **1.** Pronounce -lōō-; see ^^LU^^. **2.** The notion of fraud or underhandedness is essential to
>>>

<<<
collusion, & the following is a misuse:—The two authors, both professors at Innsbruck, appear to be working inc. ; the supposed arrangement is merely that their periods shall not overlap; in collaboration will therefore not do; if in conceré will not, the thing must be given at length.
===
collusion, & the following is a misuse:—*The two authors, both professors at Innsbruck, appear to be working in c.*; the supposed arrangement is merely that their periods shall not overlap; *in collaboration* will therefore not do; if *in concert* will not, the thing must be given at length.
>>>

<<<
COLON. See STOPS.
===
**COLON.** See ^^STOPS^^.
>>>

<<<
colossal in the sense not of enormous (as in c. folly &c.), but of indescribably
===
**colossal** in the sense not of enormous (as in *c. folly* &c.), but of indescribably
>>>

<<<
the similar use of immense, though we do not name it honoris causd, is at least of native development.
===
the similar use of *immense*, though we do not name it *honoris causâ*, is at least of native development.
>>>

<<<
colo(u)r. Keep the -u-; but see -OUR & -or. For synonymy see TINT.
===
**colo(u)r.** Keep the -u-; but see ^^-OUR^^ & ^^-OR^^. For synonymy see ^^TINT^^.
>>>

<<<
colour makes colourable, colourist, but coloration, decolorize; see -OR & -OUR, -OR- & -OUR-, HYBRID DE- RIVATIVES.
===
**colour** makes *colourable, colourist,* but *coloration, decolorize*; see ^^-OR^^ & ^^-OUR^^, ^^-OR-^^ & ^^-OUR-^^, ^^HYBRID DERIVATIVES^^.
>>>

<<<
com-. For pronunciation in commandment &c., see COL-.
===
**com-.** For pronunciation in *commandment* &c., see ^^COL-^^.
>>>

<<<
combat. Pronounce kii-. Part. & p.p. -ating, -ated; see -T-, -TT-.
===
**combat.** Pronounce kŭ-. Part. & p.p. *-ating, -ated*; see ^^-T-^^, ^^-TT-^^.
>>>

<<<
combe. See COOMB.
===
**combe.** See ^^COOMB^^.
>>>

<<<
combinedly. Four syllables if used; see -EDLY.
===
**combinedly.** Four syllables if used; see ^^-EDLY^^.
>>>

<<<
come. For c. into one's life, see HACKNEYED PHRASES.
===
**come.** For c. *into* one's *life*, see ^^HACKNEYED PHRASES^^.
>>>

<<<
come-at-able, get-at-able. Write with the hyphens. C. was made as long ago as the 17th c., but, except
===
**come-at-able, get-at-able.** Write with the hyphens. C. was made as long ago as the 17th c., but, except
>>>
